-  "body": "(Picture: The command bar open: a box saying “Type a command…” above a list of modes, each with a line on what it does, most marked generates. Caption: The command bar: type a mode, a page or an action. Rows marked generates start the AI writing.)\n\n- <kbd>⌘K</kbd> (Mac) or <kbd>Ctrl K</kbd> opens the **command bar**. Type the name of a mode, a\n  page or an action — *quotes*, *library*, *feedback* — and press <kbd>Enter</kbd>. Rows marked\n  **generates** start the AI writing something. The <kbd>⌘</kbd> button in the bottom bar opens the\n  same thing. It works while you are typing in most boxes too, and simply closing it keeps what you\n  typed. It leaves title editing and boxes inside another open dialog alone. On a Mac, use\n  <kbd>⌘K</kbd>: <kbd>Ctrl K</kbd> stays the box’s own editing key.\n- The command bar also takes a few whole requests. *where does it first mention entropy* jumps to\n  the first place the article says it. *search for entropy* or *find entropy* offers a quick search\n  for it first, on your own articles, and every place the exact word appears second. *look up\n  entropy* opens a matching term already visible in the glossary, or, once the article has a\n  glossary, offers to look it up and add it. *tag as methods* and *untag methods* change your own\n  tags, and *experimental* turns experimental features on or off. *dark mode*, *light mode* and\n  *system* change the page’s colours on this device, the choice your Profile also has. *find more\n  terms* and *more quotes* open Glossary or Quotes and press its **Find more** for you; they are\n  offered only while that list can be added to. Press the microphone in the box to say it instead of\n  typing. Press Stop twice quickly and the bar presses <kbd>Enter</kbd> for you once the words\n  arrive: it runs the command you named, or asks what you meant.\n- If you do not know the name, type or say a whole sentence — *what’s changed on this site*, *is\n  consciousness mentioned anywhere* — and, when nothing matches, press **Ask what you meant** or\n  <kbd>Enter</kbd>. A fast AI model reads your sentence and the list of commands, never the article.\n  If it is sure and the command only takes you somewhere, you go there; otherwise it shows its best\n  guesses under **Did you mean** and you press the one you want, or <kbd>Enter</kbd>. You need to be\n  signed in.\n- On your own article, once you have said why you are reading it, an empty command bar starts with\n  **Suggest what to do here**. Press it and a fast AI model reads your profile and your reason for\n  reading, never the article, and proposes a short list under **From why you’re reading**: up to\n  three quick searches, a mode or two, and a question for Chat about what the web says. Nothing is\n  done until you press a row, and each row does what it would anywhere else in the bar; the question\n  lands in Chat’s box unsent. The list stays for your visit, hides while you type, and is thrown\n  away when you edit your profile or your reason. The searches and the question are worded from what\n  you wrote, so read one before you press it.\n- <kbd>↑</kbd> / <kbd>↓</kbd> move one paragraph at a time, or one part at a time with the pointer\n  over the spine. Holding a key down does not repeat, so every step lands somewhere you can read.\n- <kbd>←</kbd> / <kbd>→</kbd> step through Skim’s stops, Quiz’s questions, Quotes’ quotes and\n  Structure’s smallest sections while that mode is open. Elsewhere they do what your browser\n  normally does.\n- In an article, the first <kbd>Tab</kbd> shows **Skip to modes**. Press <kbd>Enter</kbd> to reach\n  the mode switch in the bottom bar. Focus lands on the open mode, or the first mode button if the\n  open mode lives under **More**.\n- <kbd>G</kbd> opens the glossary at a term in the paragraph you are on; press it again for the next\n  term. <kbd>Esc</kbd> takes you back.\n- <kbd>⌘ Enter</kbd> / <kbd>Ctrl Enter</kbd> opens the article’s Metadata page, and on it takes you\n  back to the article, as pressing **Metadata** again does. In a box you are typing in — a comment,\n  feedback, a quiz answer — it saves or sends instead, and plain <kbd>Enter</kbd> starts a new line.\n- <kbd>Esc</kbd> closes whatever is in front: a dialog, a card, an open panel. On the Metadata page,\n  with nothing in front and focus outside a text box, it takes you back to the article.\n\nThe article-navigation shortcuts leave keys alone while you are typing in a box. <kbd>⌘ Enter</kbd>\n/ <kbd>Ctrl Enter</kbd> is instead left to the box to save or send, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> can still\nclose what is in front."
